    "narrative": "Today\u2019s slate for the Austin Waves is anchored by high-ceiling matchups against questionable starters, with **Drake Baldwin** (66.27) and **Kyle Tucker** (66.24) leading the offensive output. **Drake Baldwin** benefits significantly from a favorable matchup against Brandon Pfaadt and a projected spot in the #2 hole, which maximizes his exposure in a high-efficiency environment. Similarly, **Kyle Tucker** overcomes a neutral platoon situation against Robert Gasser due to a significant wind advantage, which should assist his power production. **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.** (73.66) represents our top-rated play; the combination of facing a struggling Shane Bieber and the boost from the #4 spot makes him an elite start today. Meanwhile, **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** (56.43) remains a core piece despite a difficult draw against Gerrit Cole, as his elite BvP history against the veteran right-hander (41 PA) provides a necessary floor that prevents him from sliding to the bench.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today involves **Wade Meckler** (54.93), who narrowly missed the final cut behind **Nasim Nunez** (55.16). While **Wade Meckler** provides excellent contact potential and upside in the #1 spot, the optimizer slightly favored the positional flexibility and dynamic utility role offered by **Nasim Nunez**. We are also keeping a close eye on **Gunnar Henderson** (60.09) and **Coby Mayo** (55.80); both face Steven Matz, and while Matz is a lefty, both players receive a unique \"slow delivery\" bonus that boosts our stolen base expectations\u2014a critical factor given our emphasis on speed. \n\nRisks for today center on the \"Lineup Pending\" status for almost every rostered player; should **Gunnar Henderson** or **Andy Pages** shift down in the order, their daily efficiency scores will require re-evaluation. Additionally, we are exercising caution with **Jackson Merrill** (51.51), who remains on the bench due to a challenging assignment against Gavin Williams. While **Jackson Merrill** has the talent to outperform his projection, the 2.90 xERA of his opposing pitcher makes him a clear candidate to hold in reserve to protect our remaining games-played cap.",
